TITLE = 'What Happens in the Pause'
PARAS = [
    "Think about the last time you asked a friend a difficult question. Perhaps there was a short silence before the answer came. Most of us notice such moments, but we rarely think about them. We tend to see conversation as a stream of words, and silence as the empty space between them. Yet more and more linguists* now argue that pauses are not empty at all. They carry meaning, and speakers use them, often without knowing it, to do important work.",
    "One kind of work is planning. When we speak, we are not reading from a script; we are building sentences as we go. A brief pause gives the brain time to find a word or to decide how to finish a thought. Listeners seem to {1|sense} this. When a speaker stops briefly halfway through a sentence, most people wait patiently rather than jumping in, because they understand that the sentence is not yet complete.",
    "Pauses between speakers are a different matter. In ordinary conversation, one person usually begins to talk very soon after the other stops, often in well under a second. {2|This means that listeners must start preparing their reply while the other person is still speaking, guessing how and when the turn will end.} Because the gap is normally so short, even a slightly longer silence can feel full of meaning.",
    "Imagine you invite a colleague to a party and she replies, \"Oh... I'd love to,\" but only after a pause of two seconds. The words say yes, but {3|the pause tells a different story}. Many listeners would suspect that she has another plan or simply has no wish to come. Thus a delay before an answer can soften a refusal or signal doubt, even when the words themselves sound positive.",
    "However, the meaning of silence is not the same everywhere. In some communities, a long pause before answering a serious question is a sign of respect: it shows that the listener is taking the question seriously and thinking carefully. In others, the same pause might be taken as [[4]], as if the listener had nothing to say or was not paying attention. Visitors who do not know these local habits can easily misread each other.",
    "Such misunderstandings are not limited to travel. In a classroom, for example, a teacher may ask a question and wait only a moment before calling on a student or giving the answer. Some teachers have found that when they {5|hold off} for a few extra seconds, more students raise their hands, and the answers they give tend to be longer and more thoughtful. The silence, in other words, is not wasted time; it is time in which thinking happens.",
    "Technology has added a new layer to the story. On video calls, sound and pictures may arrive a fraction of a second late. These small delays are enough to upset the natural rhythm of turn-taking*. Two people start talking together, then both stop, then both start again. {6|Many users say that online meetings leave them more tired than face-to-face ones}, and broken rhythms may be part of the reason: everyone has to work harder to judge when to speak.",
    "None of this means we should fill every gap or, at the opposite extreme, start leaving long silences on purpose. Rather, it shows that silence deserves our attention as much as words do. {7|Learning when to stay quiet, and how to read the quiet of others, may rank among the most useful communication skills we can develop.}",
]
